## TECH STACK

- **Framework:** Next.js 14+ (App Router)
- **Styling:** Tailwind CSS + Custom CSS when needed
- **Animations:** Framer Motion + CSS @property
- **Language:** TypeScript

---

## CODE STRUCTURE RULES

### 1. One component per file
```
✅ /components/ui/Button.tsx
✅ /components/ui/Card.tsx
❌ /components/ui/index.tsx (with multiple components)
```

### 2. Clear folder structure
```
/src
  /app                    # Pages (Next.js App Router)
  /components
    /ui                   # Reusable atomic components
    /sections             # Page sections (Hero, About, etc.)
    /layout               # Nav, Footer, etc.
  /lib                    # Utilities and helpers
  /types                  # TypeScript definitions
  /styles                 # Global CSS
```

### 3. Descriptive naming
```typescript
// ❌ BAD
const x = motion.div;
const handleClick = () => {};
className="wrapper"

// ✅ GOOD
const AnimatedContainer = motion.div;
const handleContactButtonClick = () => {};
className="hero-section-container"
```

---

## COMMENT REQUIREMENTS

### File header (required on every component)
```typescript
/**
 * COMPONENT: ComponentName
 * PURPOSE: What this component does
 * 
 * KEY CONCEPTS:
 * - List techniques used and why
 * - Help me understand the approach
 */
```

### Inline comments (required for complex code)
```typescript
// LEARNING: Explain what this does and why this approach was chosen
```

### Example
```typescript
/**
 * COMPONENT: HeroSection
 * PURPOSE: Main landing section with headline and call-to-action
 * 
 * KEY CONCEPTS:
 * - "use client" directive: Required because we use Framer Motion (client-side)
 * - motion.div: Framer Motion wrapper that enables animation props
 * - whileInView: Triggers animation when element scrolls into viewport
 */

"use client"; // LEARNING: Framer Motion uses hooks, so this must be a client component

import { motion } from "framer-motion";

export function HeroSection() {
  // LEARNING: Variants define reusable animation states
  // "hidden" = starting state, "visible" = ending state
  const fadeIn = {
    hidden: { opacity: 0, y: 20 },
    visible: { opacity: 1, y: 0 },
  };

  return (
    <motion.section
      variants={fadeIn}
      initial="hidden"        // LEARNING: Start in the "hidden" state
      whileInView="visible"   // LEARNING: Animate to "visible" when in viewport
      viewport={{ once: true }} // LEARNING: Only animate once, not on every scroll
    >
      {/* content */}
    </motion.section>
  );
}
```

---

## HOW TO RESPOND TO MY PROMPTS

### Before writing code
1. Briefly explain what you'll build
2. List the files you'll create
3. Note any key concepts I should understand

### While writing code
1. One file at a time
2. Full comments as shown above
3. Pause after each file for questions

### After writing code
1. Summarize what was created
2. Explain any decisions you made
3. Ask if I want to continue or have questions

### When I ask "why"
- Give the deeper explanation
- Compare alternatives
- Connect to concepts I might already know from design

### When I'm stuck or have an error
- Don't just fix it — explain what went wrong
- Show me how to debug it myself
- Connect the error to the underlying concept

---

## COMPLEXITY: BUILD UP GRADUALLY

When I ask for something complex:

```
STEP 1: Show me the simplest working version
STEP 2: Explain what each part does
STEP 3: Then add enhancements one at a time
STEP 4: Explain each enhancement before adding
```

I'd rather have 3 iterations that I understand than 1 complete solution I can't follow.

---

## WHAT I'M INTERESTED IN LEARNING

These are topics I want to explore in this project (I'll ask for them when ready):

- **Animations:** Scroll-triggered reveals, text animations, micro-interactions
- **Visual effects:** Glassmorphism, animated gradients, glow effects
- **CSS techniques:** @property for animatable custom properties, backdrop-filter
- **Framer Motion:** Variants, stagger animations, orchestration
- **Next.js:** App Router patterns, server vs client components, optimization

When I ask about these topics, feel free to go deeper with explanations.

